# Native camera forwarding and stereo microphone implementation

The operator approved proceeding through implementation and testing, stopping
only when operator input is required. Implementation from
`native-media-investigation` and its Client dependency is merged into `main`.
The measured baseline is recorded in ../investigations/native-media-forwarding.md.

## Agreed behavior

- Forward camera-native H.264/MJPEG payloads from the Ubuntu Client without a
  Client video encoder. Preserve coded payloads to Mac reception. One Mac camera
  serves compatible compressed consumers and supplies decoded pixels as required.
- Microphone audio uses stereo Opus at 192 kbps total, constrained VBR, audio
  application mode and 10 ms packets. This intentionally replaces the earlier
  native-PCM transport proposal. Preserve channel separation; do not claim
  lossless audio. Mono sources may supply the ordinary stereo capture conversion.
- The Mac virtual input exposes 48 kHz stereo float PCM. Update decoding, clock
  adaptation, shared memory and the Core Audio format together. Both channels
  follow the same frame clock and drift correction.
- Capture remains user-controlled and session-bound. Keep authentication,
  permissions, mute, disconnect, takeover, bounded queues and stale-generation
  rejection. Optional camera failure must not terminate healthy desktop media.
- New Client configurations default microphone activation to Manual; retain saved choices.
- Camera and microphone registration use an explicit endpoint allocation
  contract. Format changes and reconnects cannot reuse stale payloads.

## Required work and validation

1. Stereo audio: version the wire and launch contracts; update Client capture
   and Opus configuration, native Mac decode and virtual input. Test independent
   channel tones, malformed/old-format rejection, mute/reopen, clock drift,
   shared-buffer coherence and driver format negotiation. Compile on authorized
   builders and test the installed candidate on authorized hardware.
2. Native camera: validate physical captured H.264/MJPEG decode on the development
   Mac; implement bounded Linux capture, negotiated reverse transport and Host
   reception. Test payload equality, gaps, format/configuration bounds, keyframe
   recovery and cancellation independently of application delivery.
3. Mac camera: integrate a properly signed/provisioned camera extension and
   session-authenticated producer. Qualify coded and pixel consumers, concurrent
   readers, activation/removal and producer revocation. Only request operator
   action for actual OS permission/approval prompts or unavailable credentials.
4. Package and hardware gates: matching Host/Client builds, native video plus
   stereo audio in real applications, sustained rates, controlled loss,
   unplug/reopen, session cleanup and audio/video synchronization. Keep private
   captures, deployment identities and signing material outside Git.

The investigation commits are not implementation or hardware acceptance for
these gates. Record completed validation and remaining limitations in HANDOFF.

## Timing and concurrent-reader checkpoint

The1.1.010 implementation adds late-reader recovery-frame requests and independent
microphone3 capture timing with schema2 fallback. Bounded source timestamps follow
Opus and rendered stereo samples onto the Host camera presentation clock. See
../../../protocol/microphone.md for timing limits and fallback semantics.
Synthetic clock/queue, encrypted transport, SDK27 camera lifecycle and Ubuntu
capture tests pass. The live source timing probe passes without storing audio.
Installed simultaneous microphone readers pass stereo-format delivery checks.
On 2026-09-24, the operator confirmed distinct channels from a physical stereo
webcam microphone on the home Ubuntu Client. This qualifies audible channel
distinctness for that setup; controlled channel identity/crosstalk is unmeasured.
Sustained simultaneous native/pixel camera readers and measured camera/microphone
sync under load remain explicit hardware gates. See HANDOFF for evidence scope.
